---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Seam from '../../components/Seam.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import { t, ci } from '../../data/claims';

const crumbs = [{ label: 'Mru', href: '/' }, { label: 'Architecture' }];
---

<Base
  crumbs={crumbs}
  active="how"
  request={{ interest: 'field', heading: 'Questions about the design? Ask the person who wrote it.' }}
>
  <section class="wrap hero" style="padding-bottom: 96px">
    <Breadcrumbs items={crumbs} />
    <div class="hero-grid" style="margin-top: 40px">
      <h1 class="h-hero">Decline is the baseline.</h1>
      <div class="stack-m">
        <p class="lede">
          Most systems we build assume someone will fix them eventually. Mru starts from the opposite end: parts will
          fail, nobody will come, and the software has to keep doing useful work with whatever is left.
        </p>
        <div class="row">
          <a class="btn" href="/research/">
            Read the whitepaper
          </a>
          <a class="link-more" href="https://docs.mru.space/concepts/shrinking-quorum/">
            Technical docs
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Seam life={88} label="01 · The assumption" />

  <section class="wrap section">
    <div class="split">
      <div class="stack-m">
        <p class="eyebrow">The intervention assumption</p>
        <h2 class="h2">Every long-lived system is a maintained system.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="stack-m">
        <p>
          Voyager has flown for 49 years, but a team on Earth still nurses it. Its power drops about 4 watts a year, and
          people decide what to switch off next.
        </p>
        <p class="soft">
          Mru works on the case where the people are taken away, on Earth and in space. Voyager&#39;s own limit is
          power, which Mru does not address today.
        </p>
        <div class="cards" style="margin-top: 32px">
          <div class="stat">
            <div class="v">
              49<small>years</small>
            </div>
            <p class="l">Voyager in flight, with a ground team the whole time.</p>
            <p class="src">
              <a href="https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/news/nasa-turns-off-two-voyager-science-instruments-to-extend-mission/">
                NASA JPL, 2025
              </a>
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="stat">
            <div class="v">
              4<small>W / year</small>
            </div>
            <p class="l">Voyager&#39;s power loss, about 4 W a year. Instruments go dark one by one.</p>
            <p class="src">
              <a href="https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/news/nasa-turns-off-two-voyager-science-instruments-to-extend-mission/">
                NASA JPL, 2025
              </a>
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Seam life={76} label="02 · The principle" />

  <section class="wrap section">
    <div class="split rev">
      <div class="stack-m">
        <p class="eyebrow">The Degradation-First Principle</p>
        <p class="quote">
          &#8220;Capability is a depleting resource to be spent, not a fixed budget to be defended.&#8221;
        </p>
        <p class="quote-by">Whitepaper, §5 · Corollary</p>
      </div>
      <div class="stack-m">
        <p>
          Most fault-tolerant designs treat a failure as an exception: detect it, mask it, get back to normal. On a
          system nobody can repair, there is no normal to get back to.
        </p>
        <p>
          So Mru treats its own decay, of power, computing, memory and heat, as the governing condition. The goal is not
          to keep full function. The goal is the most useful work across the whole descent, from full capability to
          silence.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Seam life={64} label="03 · The quorum" />

  <section class="wrap section stack-l">
    <div class="split">
      <div class="stack-m">
        <p class="eyebrow">The shrinking quorum</p>
        <h2 class="h2">Same hardware. Two designs.</h2>
      </div>
      <p class="lede">
        Fixed triple modular redundancy (TMR) votes on three processors and stops when it can no longer form a majority.
        Mru keeps going: vote on three, compare on two, check its own work on one.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="tbl-box" tabindex="0">
      <table class="tbl" style="font-size: 18px">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Processors left</th>
            <th>Fixed TMR</th>
            <th>Mru</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">3</td>
            <td>Vote. One bad result is outvoted.</td>
            <td>Vote. One bad result is outvoted.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">2</td>
            <td>Compare. A mismatch loses the result.</td>
            <td>Compare. A known-answer test finds the bad one.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="hl">
            <td class="m">1</td>
            <td style="color: var(--ink-faint)">Stopped, for good.</td>
            <td>Still working, at half speed. It computes twice and checks itself.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="tiny">
      Kani proves that, with two or more replicas alive, the decision function gives the same outcome under both
      policies, for every set of three replies. Over a whole run, never less than fixed TMR is tested, not proved: Mru
      also retires replicas that fixed TMR keeps.{' '}
      <a href="https://docs.mru.space/concepts/shrinking-quorum/">The rule and its proofs</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section" style="padding-top: 0" id="prior-art">
    <div class="split">
      <div class="stack-m">
        <p class="eyebrow">Prior art</p>
        <h2 class="h2">Stepping down is not new.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="stack-m">
        <p>
          Majority voting over replicas goes back to von Neumann in 1956. The Space Shuttle&#39;s computers voted, then
          compared and self-tested with two left. Spacecraft fault protection has acted on its own for decades, then
          waited for the ground.
        </p>
        <p class="soft">
          Mru adds three things: it degrades on its own with no ground team, its decision core is small and verified,
          and its trade data is public.
        </p>
        <a class="link-more" href="https://docs.mru.space/concepts/prior-art/">
          Prior art and related work, with sources
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section" style="padding-top: 0" id="open-questions">
    <div class="split">
      <div class="stack-m">
        <p class="eyebrow">Open questions</p>
        <h2 class="h2">What is not solved yet.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="tbl-box" tabindex="0">
        <table class="tbl">
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td style="width: 32%">Common-mode faults</td>
              <td>
                Shared power, clock or heat can fail every replica at once. Dusk models correlated deaths, and the demo
                models one upset that hits both self-check runs. Neither models a shared power supply or clock.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Input agreement</td>
              <td>
                The demo derives each input from the tick, so replicas agree by construction. Real sensor inputs need an
                agreement step between replicas. Mru does not provide one, and nothing models it.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Replica determinism</td>
              <td>
                The demo task is a pure function of the tick. Interrupts and scheduling on real flight software can make
                healthy replicas disagree. Neither Dusk nor the bench models that.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Known-answer test coverage</td>
              <td>
                Measured for the demo task on general-purpose processors, on the bench. Not measured on target hardware
                or under radiation.
              </td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Seam life={52} label="04 · The architecture" />

  <section class="wrap section stack-l">
    <div class="split">
      <div class="stack-m">
        <p class="eyebrow">Six layers</p>
        <h2 class="h2">Six layers. The lower the layer, the smaller and more verified.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="stack-m">
        <p>
          The whitepaper resolves a paradox: a system must be simple enough to be correct, and complex enough to decide
          things alone. The answer is layers. The lower the layer, the smaller and more verified it is.
        </p>
        <p class="soft small">
          This is the full architecture from the whitepaper. Today the software implements the Layer 1 quorum and its
          health record.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="strata">
      <div class="stratum">
        <span class="lv">L5</span>
        <h3>Bounded self-change</h3>
        <p>
          A constitution in read-only memory. Rules can adapt how the mission is pursued, never what the mission is.
          Every change can roll back.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="stratum">
        <span class="lv">L4</span>
        <h3>Communication</h3>
        <p>Coding that adapts as power falls, from kilobits per second to bits per hour, and at the end a beacon.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="stratum">
        <span class="lv">L3</span>
        <h3>Science and knowledge triage</h3>
        <p>Rule-based, not neural networks: legible and bounded. Decides what is worth keeping as storage decays.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="stratum">
        <span class="lv">L2</span>
        <h3>Navigation</h3>
        <p>Star tracking with a fallback to dead reckoning.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="stratum">
        <span class="lv">L1</span>
        <h3>Hardware consensus and health</h3>
        <p>
          The shrinking quorum, a health record, memory scrubbing and a power accountant. This is the core that Mru
          Field and Mru Flight are built on.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="stratum">
        <span class="lv">L0</span>
        <h3>Survival kernel</h3>
        <p>
          Design targets: about 200 to 500 instructions, under 10,000 lines of verified code, under half a watt in
          hibernation. Its job is to keep the system alive.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Seam life={40} label="05 · The descent" />

  <section class="wrap section stack-l">
    <div class="split">
      <div class="stack-m">
        <p class="eyebrow">Power phases</p>
        <h2 class="h2">Defined behavior at each power level.</h2>
      </div>
      <p class="lede">
        Power draw spans nearly three orders of magnitude. The system knows which phase it is in and spends what is left
        on purpose.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="tbl-box" tabindex="0">
      <table class="tbl">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Phase</th>
            <th>Power</th>
            <th>What it does</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Full operations</td>
            <td class="m">50–200 W</td>
            <td>Everything runs. Full voting.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Reduced</td>
            <td class="m">20–80 W</td>
            <td>Fewer instruments, less often.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Core operations</td>
            <td class="m">5–20 W</td>
            <td>Only the essential job.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Survival</td>
            <td class="m">1–5 W</td>
            <td>Stay alive. Wake rarely.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="hl">
            <td>Beacon</td>
            <td class="m">&lt;1 W</td>
            <td>Heartbeat only: a periodic low-rate beacon that reports the system is alive.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="tiny">Phases from the whitepaper, §13. A probe adds a Transit phase (&lt;1 W) before arrival.</p>
  </section>

  <Seam life={28} label="06 · The proof" />

  <section class="wrap section">
    <div class="split">
      <div class="stack-m">
        <p class="eyebrow">Evidence</p>
        <h2 class="h2">Tested where it can be.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="stack-m">
        <p>
          We test in simulation and on a bench, in the open. Field and flight tests are proposed, not done. Dusk ran{' '}
          {t('dusk.runs')} missions on hardware that only ever decays, with no ground support after launch. The
          shrinking quorum delivered {t('dusk.work_vs_tmr')} the useful work of fixed TMR (95% CI{' '}
          {ci('dusk.work_vs_tmr')}). With ground support that never ends, the gain is {t('dusk.ground.never')}. Against
          three-string cold standby it ranges from {t('dusk.work_vs_cold.01')} to {t('dusk.work_vs_cold.10')}, depending
          on how fast an unpowered spare ages.
        </p>
        <div class="row">
          <a class="btn" href="/research/dusk-results/">
            See the results
          </a>
          <a class="link-more" href="/field/">
            Mru Field
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
